    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, I'm prepping a demo for my chemistry class about why cutting onions causes that stinging sensation in your eyes. Could you walk me through the specific chemical pathway and the enzymatic reaction that leads to the formation of the volatile compounds responsible for the irritation?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! When you cut an onion, you rupture its cells, releasing enzymes like allinase that break down sulfur-containing amino acids into compounds such as sulfenic acid. Sulfenic acid rapidly rearranges to form thiosulfinic acid, which further converts into allicin and propanethial S-oxide. Propanethial S-oxide is a volatile compound that quickly dissolves in the moisture of your eyes, forming sulfuric acid that irritates the ocular tissue when it's dissolved in your tears. The sensation is temporary as enzymes like phenolase may partially occur to detoxify the irritant.",
